// A Void-returning call reassigned into an already-declared Void-typed local (`m =
// nothing()`) lowers through the ordinary Assignment path, which builds `target = value;`
// unconditionally. The call's own C return type is `void`, so that store is illegal C
// ("invalid use of void expression") -- the same shape `let x = nothing()` was fixed for
// (lower_let's Void arm), just reached through Assignment instead of LetBinding. The
// target already holds the int64_t placeholder its own `let` gave it, and Void has
// exactly one value, so nothing needs to be written back; only the call's side effect has
// to run.
//
// `m += nothing()` is a related shape but not this ticket's: typecheck currently accepts
// `+=` on a Void operand at all (a hole, since arithmetic traits are sealed to the
// numerics), and a codegen arm here would paper over that instead of the typecheck fix
// closing it. tests/test_296hpm_void_let_binding.bl carries that row and stays red until
// the typecheck fix lands.
//
// The same shape recurs where a match arm's tail value is stored into a dest temp
// (TAIL_ASSIGN): a diverging arm whose tail is a Void-returning call (`env.exit`) has
// nothing to store either, since dest_tid there is the match's own type (decided by a
// sibling arm), not this arm's.

fn nothing() { }

fn reassign_void() -> Int {
    let mut m = nothing()
    m = nothing()
    5
}

fn diverging_arm_tail_call(n: Int) -> Int {
    let r = match n {
        0 => env.exit(9)
        _ => 2
    }
    r
}

test "reassigning a Void-typed local through a genuine void call compiles and runs" {
    assert_eq(reassign_void(), 5)
}

test "a braceless diverging arm whose call is Void-typed keeps the sibling arm value" {
    assert_eq(diverging_arm_tail_call(1), 2)
}
